<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Chaffee, J. W. (1985) The Thorny Gates of Learning in Sung China: A Social History of Examinations. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Chaffee, J. W. (1999) Branches of Heaven: A History of the Imperial Clan of Sung China. Cambridge, MA: Harvard University Asia Center.</li><li>Ebrey, P. B. (1993) The Inner Quarters: Marriage and the Lives of Chinese Women in the Sung Period. Berkeley: University of California Press.</li><li>Hymes, R. P. (1986) Statesmen and Gentlemen: The Elite of Fu-chou, Chiang-hsi, in Northern and Southern Sung. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Kuhn, D. (2009) The Age of Confucian Rule: The Song Transformation of China. Cambridge, MA: Belknap Press of Harvard University Press.</li><li>Twitchett, D. and Smith, P. J. (eds) (2009) The Cambridge History of China, Volume 5, Part One: The Sung Dynasty and Its Precursors, 907–1279. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: about 1100, when the Northern Song census recorded some 20 million households; modern estimates put the population at about 90 million, others at more than 100 million.</p></section>
